class Solution {
  public int prefixStencilsBossReceipt(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int[]sums=new int[a.length+1];for(int i=0;i<a.length;i++)sums[i+1]=sums[i]+a[i];for(int i=0;i<a.length;i++)out.add(sums[i+1]-sums[Math.max(0,i-k+1)]);}
    int answer=0;for(int i=0;i<out.size();i++)answer+=(i+1)*out.get(i);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build rangeSums

For each position in `readings`, add the current number and up to `k` - 1 numbers just before it. Stop at the start of the input if needed. Record each sum, from left to right. For `readings` = [2, 3, 4] and `k` = 2, `rangeSums` is [2, 5, 7].

Return the answer

Multiply each number in `rangeSums` by its position, counting from 1: the first number by 1, the second by 2, and so on. Add those products. Store the sum in `weightedTotal`. Complete `prefixStencilsBossReceipt` so it returns `weightedTotal`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `rangeSums` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

